{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small services is more resilient to correlated failure than a large redundant service because it replaces 'hardware redundancy' with 'operational diversity', which breaks the single-plane failure mode.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services all rely on a single 'industry standard' library with a critical vulnerability (the 'Common Library Trap').","The services share a common underlying infrastructure provider (e.g., all hosted on one cloud region).","The network lacks sufficient capacity to handle the redistribution of load when a subset of nodes fails."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"In a large service, internal redundancy (e.g., 100 identical nodes) is typically managed by a unified control plane or deployment pipeline. A single 'poison pill' configuration or orchestration bug creates a correlated failure across all redundant units. In a decentralized network, diverse deployment pipelines, different OS versions, and varied operator behaviors ensure that a single administrative or logical error has a limited 'blast radius', preventing total system collapse.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 'Recovery Signature' (synchronized recovery) is the primary evidence required to distinguish truly independent failures from those that are merely apparently uncorrelated.","counterconditions":["Recovery is triggered by a global signal (e.g., a DNS update) that is independent of the original failure cause.","The 'Recovery Wave' is an artifact of a shared automated health-check interval rather than a shared cause."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Failures that appear uncorrelated (staggered in time and space) may actually be caused by a shared external constraint. If these failures recover in a tight, synchronized window (a 'Recovery Wave'), it indicates they were all awaiting the resolution of a single shared cause. Truly independent failures will exhibit staggered recovery times reflecting the unique local conditions of each independent operator.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A large service with high internal redundancy is actually *more* prone to catastrophic correlated failure due to the 'Complexity Paradox'.","counterconditions":["The orchestration layer is designed with formal verification or extreme simplicity.","The decentralized network's lack of coordination leads to 'management debt' and configuration drift that triggers cascades."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"To manage massive internal redundancy, a service must implement an increasingly complex orchestration and coordination layer. This layer creates tight feedback loops and systemic dependencies. The orchestration layer itself becomes a single point of failure; as redundancy increases, the complexity of the 'manager' increases, making the system more susceptible to metastable failure states that are impossible in a loosely coupled decentralized network.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are less resilient than large redundant services because they suffer from 'Logical Monoliths' despite operational diversity.","counterconditions":["The decentralized network intentionally mandates implementation diversity (e.g., different languages for the same function).","The large service's unified deployment pipeline is the primary vector for the failure."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"While operators may be independent, they converge on the same 'best-of-breed' software stacks (e.g., specific versions of Linux, Nginx, or Java). This creates a hidden layer of homogeneity. A vulnerability in a common dependency (e.g., Log4j) acts as a correlated trigger that bypasses operational diversity, whereas a large service can deploy a single, coordinated patch across its entire redundant fleet faster than a decentralized network can coordinate diverse operators.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The resilience of a decentralized network is capped by a 'Diversity-Efficiency Trade-off' where too much diversity increases the attack surface for correlated failures.","counterconditions":["Security patching is decoupled from implementation diversity.","The cost of exploiting diverse systems is significantly higher than exploiting a monolith."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Maintaining a truly diverse set of implementations increases the 'security debt' of the network. Operators running niche or old versions to maintain diversity may leave unpatched vulnerabilities. A sophisticated attacker can exploit these varied weaknesses in a coordinated strike, turning the very diversity intended for resilience into a map of exploitable entry points that lead to a correlated collapse.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Speculating on the 'Fragility of Coordination': In the large service, the 'active-active dynamic traffic evacuation' mentioned in SRC_003 is a double-edged sword. While it mitigates local failure, the *evacuation mechanism itself* can cause a correlated failure via a 'thundering herd' effect, where the act of saving one region kills the others. A decentralized network might be 'resilient' simply because it is too fragmented to support such a high-speed, systemic contagion.","why_keep_this":"Connects the redundancy argument to systemic dynamics (thundering herd)."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we define a mathematical 'Diversity Coefficient' that quantifies when the benefit of operational diversity outweighs the cost of management debt?","related_refs":[]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}